::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which opening best fits an oral presentation to school leaders? {
=A. Today I will explain why a supervised after-school study space would support students who remain on campus. #Correct. This opening identifies purpose, audience, and topic clearly.
~B. I am going to talk about some school stuff. #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~C. You should all agree with me right away. #Incorrect. This is not respectful or evidence-based.
~D. My presentation might be about a few things. #Incorrect. This lacks focus.
}

::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which digital slide choice is clearest? {
~A. Tiny numbers on a busy background #Incorrect. The audience may not be able to read the evidence.
=B. A clear title, readable chart, and labeled survey result #Correct. These features help the audience understand the evidence.
~C. A decorative picture with no labels #Incorrect. Decoration does not explain the claim.
~D. A full script in small font #Incorrect. A slide should support the speaker, not overload the audience.
}

::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which detail is the best evidence? {
~A. Students probably need a quiet place. #Incorrect. This is a guess.
~B. A study space sounds useful. #Incorrect. This is an opinion.
=C. 68 of 120 surveyed students reported staying after school at least twice a week. #Correct. This is specific survey evidence.
~D. People said things about school. #Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence uses standard English conventions? {
~A. Students needs a quiet study space. #Incorrect. The plural subject needs the verb "need."
~B. Because students remain after school. #Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~C. Students need, a quiet study space. #Incorrect. The comma is unnecessary and incorrect.
=D. Students need a quiet study space after school. #Correct. This sentence is complete and correct.
}

::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which phrase is most formal? {
=A. supervised academic workspace #Correct. This phrase is precise and appropriate for school leaders.
~B. cool place to chill #Incorrect. This is casual and not academic.
~C. some school thing #Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. a bunch of stuff #Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
}

::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What should a speaker do with important evidence during delivery? {
~A. Skip it because it is on the slide. #Incorrect. Evidence should be explained orally.
=B. Explain what the evidence means and how it supports the claim. #Correct. Oral delivery should interpret evidence for the audience.
~C. Read it as fast as possible. #Incorrect. Speed can hurt understanding.
~D. Replace it with a joke. #Incorrect. That would weaken the academic message.
}

::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best fits school leaders as the audience? {
~A. This plan is awesome for kids. #Incorrect. This is informal and imprecise.
~B. This thing helps people. #Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. This proposal would support students who need a supervised place to complete academic work. #Correct. This revision is formal, specific, and audience-aware.
~D. This idea is cool because it is cool. #Incorrect. This is circular and informal.
}

::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which action shows readiness to submit? {
~A. Submit without checking speaker notes #Incorrect. Notes need review.
~B. Change colors without checking content #Incorrect. Visual changes alone are not enough.
~C. Ignore feedback because the presentation is finished #Incorrect. Feedback should guide revision.
=D. Check that slides, notes, evidence, tone, and conventions align #Correct. This confirms publication readiness.
}

::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Why should a slide avoid full paragraphs? {
=A. Full paragraphs can overload the audience and distract from the speaker's explanation. #Correct. Slides should highlight key information clearly.
~B. Slides should never contain words. #Incorrect. Focused words can help.
~C. The audience should not see evidence. #Incorrect. Evidence should be visible.
~D. Full paragraphs always prove the claim. #Incorrect. More text does not equal stronger reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U06_PRE_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaker note best explains evidence? {
~A. This number is here. #Incorrect. This does not explain meaning.
=B. Because more than half of surveyed students stay after school, the proposal addresses a need that affects many students. #Correct. This explains the evidence and connects it to the claim.
~C. The slide already says it. #Incorrect. The speaker still needs to interpret the evidence.
~D. I like this number. #Incorrect. This is personal opinion, not explanation.
}
